The answer is: ...came to Oyan.  They were on a mission o'witchcraft, and they had stumbled on Witches' Alley, the path that leads to the witches' island.  The men from Egulac were not foolish: they knew that if they went any further they would surely die.  So they stood by the side of the path and waited.  When a witch passes by, they killed her.  Thus they continued, killing seven witches, until they had killed all the witches on the island.  Then they returned to their canoes and paddled away.  The morale of the story is: Don't mess with witches.  Or something like that.

The story of One Night Two Men from Egulac is told in the book Native Tales of the Pacific Northwest by Franz Boas (1894).  The story is classified as a creation story because it explains the origin of the Ha-ha-ha-ha people (Westergaard 1989).  In the story, the two men are identified as Ha-ha-ha-ha people (Boas 1894).  The people are said to have lived at one time on the mainland, but were driven by the cruelty of the people on the island to abandon their homes and migrate to the San Juan Islands (Boas 1894).  The people are thought to be related to the Saanich and Tlingit (Boas 1894).


