As part of the Innis brothers' plan to establish a fur trading empire, they had built a set of seven forts along the upper Missinippi River (later known as the Nelson River) in what is now Manitoba. The forts were meant to serve as trading posts and to assert Canadian sovereignty over the region. The Innis brothers' plan was outlined in a document called the Cousin's Fur Trade Plan. The forts were constructed from 1821 to 1825.From west to east, the forts were named Forts Reliance, Reserve, Scaterd, St. Charles, Assiniboia, Champlain, and Nottawind.The two men from Egulac referred to in the opening line of the poem are Fur trader Thomas Tait and his Cree partner, Guillaume Couturier. In 1840, Tait and Couturier built a fort at the site of present-day Winnipeg. They called it Fort Aberdeen. Before building the fort, Tait and Couturier had built a house for Couturier's sister, Ellen, and her husband, Andrew Blanchet. This house was the firstframe dwelling built in the region. The couple lived there with their son, Joseph, who later became the first Catholic bishop of Winnipeg.The poem goes on to tell how Tait and Couturier, after spending the winter of 1840–1841 at their new fort, traveled down the Nelson River in canoes to meet with other fur traders at Fort John. On their way back upriver, they were met with a shock: the once-frozen Nelson River was now open water. The unexpected thaw caused by global warming gave rise to the following verse:

            And soon the winter's work is done,
            The rivers run with melting tide;

