According to one source, "One Night Two Men from Egulac" is a Canadian legend about two Cree hunters who encountered a witch.The legend is told in the book "Canadian Folktales", edited by Ingram Budd, and was collected from unnamed "Indian informants" in 1922.The story begins with a description of the natural beauty of the Egulac region in autumn. Then two men from the Nadouessi band of the Cree Nation go out to hunt. They follow the tracks of a giant moose through the woods. Suddenly they hear strange noises and see lights in the distance. They approach a clearing and see a witch standing there. She invites them to a feast. The men are puzzled but accept the invitation. They sit down to eat and drink with the witch. Eventually the witch transforms into an old woman and tells the men that they will soon die. One of the men asks the witch why she hates them so much. The witch tells him that it is because he shot an arrow into her wooden canoe. The man realizes that the witch is really a spirit from the water. The men make their escape and return to their camp. They build a big fire and tell the other villagers about their encounter with the water spirit. From that night on, the fire is kept burning.The legend does not have a clear moral or message, but it does reflect the Cree worldview and relationship with the natural world. The story also demonstrates the importance of oral tradition and storytelling in Cree culture.


